%N A400436 Integers that can be expressed as the sum of 5 factorials.
%C A400436 The subsequence for sums of 5 distinct factorials starts 153, 753, 849, 867, 871, 872, 5073,... - _R. J. Mathar_, Oct 08 2026
%H A400436 Michael B. Porter, <a href="/A400436/b400436.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A400436 75 is in the sequence because 75 = 4! + 4! + 4! + 2! + 1!.
%o A400436 (PARI) {my(P = sum(n=0, 5, x^(n!)), limit = 134); print(select(v -> polcoeff(P^5, v) > 0, [1..limit]))}
%Y A400436 Cf. A000142, A066847 (2 factorials), A400435 (3 factorials), A111222 (4 factorials).
